  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/0/06/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_10.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A new character is on the scene, claiming to be the head of the crime syndicate - the \"Big Man.\"&nbsp; Any who object to it will answer to his Enforcers, which consist of Fancy Dan, a master of judo and karate, the Ox, a slow, lumbering brute of a man, and Montana, an expert with the lasso.&nbsp; Nobody better mess with the Big Man!\n\nMeanwhile, at the Forest Hills hospital, Peter is surprised to see Liz and Flash stopping by to visit the recovering Aunt May, who they heard about.&nbsp; Flash is only along under protest.&nbsp; The doctor tells Peter that his aunt is recovering but that she needs a blood transfusion.&nbsp; Peter is worried about how his radiated blood might affect Aunt May, but as Liz and Flash are there watching, he has no choice but to agree, and the deed is done, but he'll be somewhat weakened for a bit.&nbsp; Aunt May soon heads south to recover, but is worried about Peter.&nbsp; (Awfully convenient that his blood type happens to match Aunt May's given that she was only <i>married</i> to his actual blood relative Ben Parker - but this is one of those cases where it's probably best not to overthink it...)\n\nBut a new crime wave begins to sweep the city, organized under the direction of the Big Man!&nbsp; Anyone who refuses to join their mob operation answers to the Enforcers!&nbsp; And the small fry who are captured by the police know that the Big Man will arrange for their release in short order.&nbsp; The police are doing what they can, but Spider-Man looks for clues as to who the Big Man is.\n\nAt the same time, J. Jonah Jameson isn't a believer in the Big Man, thinking that it's a character invented by Spider-Man.&nbsp; He heads back to the Bugle and orders his ace columnist, Frederick Foswell, to write a \"series of articles which will <i>prove</i> that the Big Man is none other than <i>Spider-Man</i> himself!\"&nbsp; (Giving this columnist a name - we don't learn his <i>first</i> name until page 12 - should be clue enough for any astute readers out there - any time a low-level character is introduced and immediately given a name, we should be suspicious!&nbsp; See [LINK:1962:FF_12|FF #12|2026-05-01] for another example, the one which guest-stars the Hulk.)&nbsp; Foswell is hesitant, reminding Jonah that he claimed just last month that Electro and Spider-Man were the same person, but Jameson will have none of it!\n\nOut in the office, Betty Brant is leaving for the day, and once outside, is met by the Enforcers, who demand the \"rest of the money you owe!\"&nbsp; Peter happens to walk by just then, and is roughed up by the Ox to show Betty they mean business.&nbsp; Pay up or pay the consequences.&nbsp; Betty is too embarrassed to admit to Peter that she borrowed money from a loan shark (why, we're not told).\n\nBefore the Enforcers get far, they're tracked down by Spider-Man!&nbsp; Finally, he's face-to-face with the Big Man and his Enforcers!&nbsp; A couple of pages are used up by a brief struggle, before Spider-Man begins to weaken because he hasn't fully recovered from the blood transfusion.&nbsp; He slips away in darkness and is surprised to see J. Jonah Jameson walking away from the building.&nbsp; He reasons that the Big Man must be someone with money, someone well connected, but <i>nah</i>, it <i>couldn't </i>be Jonah, could it?\n\nPeter goes home and calls Betty to learn more, but she is terrified he might get hurt if he gets involved with the Enforcers, and to protect him, she leaves town!&nbsp; Peter finds she's not at the Bugle office the next day.&nbsp; He bumps into Foswell, who doesn't believe that Spider-Man is the Big Man any more than Peter does, but he gets paid to write what Jameson wants!&nbsp; Peter is discouraged since the Enforcers are scaring all the small-time crooks so badly, none of them are turning in the Big Man.\n\nAs Spider-Man, he's made no progress, but perhaps as Peter Parker, he might arrange to have himself kidnapped and brought before the Big Man!&nbsp; So he announces the next day at school that he's figured out who the Big Man is, and his reputation as a brainiac makes it sound plausible enough.&nbsp; Even Flash pulls Peter aside and tells him that his life won't be worth a nickel if the Enforcers hear about what he thinks he knows.&nbsp; \"I didn't think you cared!\" is what Peter tells Flash (<i>heh</i>).\n\nBig Man is tipped off that Peter Parker knows who the Big Man is, and we learn that the Big Man also knows who Peter Parker is!&nbsp; He orders that he be brought in.&nbsp; The Enforcers intercept Parker and force him into a car to be led before the Big Man.&nbsp; Peter is elated - his plan is working!&nbsp; But he's surprised to learn that the Big Man knows him!&nbsp; Could it be Jonah after all?\n\nThey drive to a large, indoor auto-parking building and shove him into a locked room, giving him the opportunity to change to Spider-Man, who soon finds out that \"every top mobster in the city\" is on hand!&nbsp; Something big is about to happen!&nbsp; And it's too many for even Spider-Man to handle, but he's spotted by the Enforcers and is soon thrust into battle, where we spend the next 2-3 pages before Spider-Man is able to summon the police for help.\n\nAs the noose tightens around the Big Man, he tries to escape with Spider-Man following!&nbsp; But Spider-Man, still somewhat weakened, isn't fast enough to catch him before he slips away.&nbsp; But now he thinks he knows where the Big Man is and heads straight for the Daily Bugle.&nbsp; He finds Jameson nervously talking to Foswell.&nbsp; Suddenly, the police show up and say they've found the Big Man.&nbsp; Spider-Man, looking through the window is saddened - he didn't think Jonah was really bad, but here the police are, but <i>wait!&nbsp; </i>They're not <i>after</i> Jameson.&nbsp; It's Foswell they want - they found evidence in his car, which was seen speeding away from the garage!\n\n\"I'd have gotten away with it <i>too</i>...if not for some crummy luck!\"&nbsp; (<i>what?&nbsp; No \"meddling kids?\"&nbsp; Heh</i>)&nbsp; Spider-Man is stunned.&nbsp; It was Foswell all along!\n\nJameson is crushed, and as he stands there, embarrassed again by Spider-Man, finally admits (to the reader) that he's actually jealous of Spider-Man, who \"risks his life day after day with no thought of reward!&nbsp; If a man like him is good - is a hero - then what am <i>I</i>?\"&nbsp; Spider-Man represents everything that's not - brave, powerful and unselfish.&nbsp; He has to keep trying to find a way of tearing him down because he actually envies him!\n\nMeanwhile, Peter gets a card from Aunt May in Florida - she's doing fine.&nbsp; But nothing from Betty.&nbsp; And we see that she's in a locked hotel room in Pennsylvania, weeping, missing Peter terribly.&nbsp; She can't risk his life!\n\nNext issue we're promised to learn the \"strange secret of Betty Brant.\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "Probably the most well-crafted plot we've seen in a Marvel comic to date.&nbsp; We're kept guessing until the final couple of pages about who the Big Man is.&nbsp; We even almost believe that Jameson might actually <i>be</i> the Big Man, and we can't believe that any more than Spider-Man does!&nbsp; The supporting characters are all there, and all are given good roles.&nbsp; Peter uses his brain to play a part in tracking down the Big Man.&nbsp; We're left wondering how Betty got involved with a loan shark.&nbsp; The Enforcers aren't particularly great in their own right, but they're only intended to be a deterrent for small-time crooks, not to be a genuine menace to a super-hero like Spider-Man, and as such, they play their roles well.\n\nSpider-Man's banter was in character and is not only improving but now becoming <i>expected</i>.&nbsp; He's always filled the air with snide remarks, but with this issue, and against so many, he really lets loose with it.&nbsp; I feel Stan Lee is here really getting him into character.\n\nTo my knowledge, this is the first time we see organized crime so vividly portrayed in the comics (at least in <i>Marvel</i> comics - one might imagine that possibly it cropped up in DC titles such as <i>Batman</i> before, but I don't know this for a fact).&nbsp; It later becomes such a hallmark of books such as the Spider-Man titles and Daredevil that we really forget where it began.&nbsp; It's perfectly suited for \"street-level\" characters such as Spider-Man, and this issue's handling of it, while admittedly still in its early stages, did a solid job with its portrayal.&nbsp; A team such as the Enforcers is in character for a mob boss such as the Big Man.\n\nIf there are downsides to this issue at all, it's that 1) the cover is rather uninspired and has no background images at all, meaning that if I wanted to slab an early issue of ASM, I would <i>never</i> choose this one, 2) we're never told who Aunt May went to Florida with and 3) that it was all so tidily wrapped up in a single issue.&nbsp; This sort of thing could have been extended into multiple issues, and we only get a little bit of that with the dangling plot thread of Betty Brant, which makes us want the next issue <i>now</i>!&nbsp; It all feels like a warm-up for what will eventually do just this with the upcoming Green Goblin.&nbsp; But it is what it is, and these aspects drop it below NM in my mind.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis issue is to be applauded.&nbsp; It was the best issue of Spider-Man to date (edging out issue 3), and all of that with no real super-villain, which is even <i>more</i> impressive!&nbsp; An early triumph for Marvel Comics, and I whole-heartedly award it a high-grade rating!&nbsp; <i>Fabulously done!</i>\n\n(Curiously though, the <i>Announcements</i> section of [LINK:1963:FF_24|FF #24|2027-04-08] indicates that Spider-Man fights \"<i>FIVE</i> foes at once!\"&nbsp; Hmm.. a miscount perhaps?&nbsp; Did I miss someone?)\n\n<b>FIXME</b> - though the magazine itself does not refer yet to Spider-Man as \"Spidey,\" (and you'll note I've avoided using the term <i>because</i> of that), the Special Announcement in this issue's letters page <i>does</i>.&nbsp; See the Dec 1963 monthly summary for more details.\n\n<i>Update on 7/15/2023:</i>  The letters page of ASM #14 had a letter by Jim Civarra about this issue that I thought summed it up nicely.&nbsp; Some excerpts:\n<i>This is the type of story Spider-Man was made for.&nbsp; There was no trite villain (I don't care what anyone says - comics didn't need super-villains before and don't need 'em now); the story was a real mystery masterpiece (I was shocked at the ending, as I thought Jameson was the \"Big Man\"); and last but not least, some really good earthly criminal crimes were committed.&nbsp; There was no evil chemist bent on world destruction or an alien from outer space slopping up the plot.</i>\n(Nicely put - this story still ranks in my mind as the best-crafted plot I've encountered so far - I <i>know</i> that will change, but at this early stage, it's the truth.)",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}